WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.490
Anyone can run code to get a fast answer,
but true data science requires knowing what to ask.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.090
National University's Ana 6:05 Analytic Models course
bridges that gap.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.650
Here are four vital tools
you'll master.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.490
First up, multivariable regression.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.970
Real-world data is messy, full of confounding
factors that blur the truth.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.630
This tool mathematically controls for that noise,
isolating the true effect of each individual variable.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:28.870
Next, bootstrapping.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.670
If you have a limited data set,
this technique repeatedly resamples your observed data.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:40.550
This builds a robust distribution,
letting you generate highly reliable predictions

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:40.550
without needing massive amounts of information.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.150
Third, confidence intervals.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:49.430
Instead of offering one definitive point estimate,
they provide a calculated range of plausible values.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:56.190
This mathematically quantifies exactly
how much uncertainty exists,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:56.190
giving business leaders the confidence
to make safe decisions.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.070
Finally, transparent communication.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.110
A statistical model is useless
if your stakeholders cannot understand it.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:08.110
You will learn to translate raw outputs
like residual testing

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.510
into clear, actionable, and peer-reviewed
intelligence.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.870
By combining advanced statistical modeling
with transparent communication,

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:17.870
you evolve from simply running code
into a strategic data leader.